title: Cancelling a task
tags: Tasks
kind: guide
summary: Stop a reconcile, a scan, a query or a flush; what stopping each one leaves behind.

```
mf task show <id> --stop     (or --cancel)
```

or the ''Stop'' button next to the task in the `repos` panel. A task says whether
it can be stopped (`cancellable` in [[Task endpoints]]). Stopping is cooperative:
the task stops at its next checkpoint, so the status reads `cancelled` a moment
after the request.

|!Kind |!Stopping it |
|`reconcile` |stops at its next progress step and drops its transaction: the repository is exactly as before the reconcile started — there is no partial reconcile |
|`duplicates` |keeps what it computed: the hashes already committed and the groups of the largest files, found first; only the pruning of stale groups is skipped |
|`relink` |keeps the pairings it already committed |
|`query` |stops within a few hundred keys read, whatever it was doing; the waiting client gets a `409` (`query cancelled`) instead of results |
|`flush` |stops at the next event and drops the half-applied group (groups already committed stay). Nothing is lost — the events go back to the buffer — and the repository's tracking is left ''paused'', or the next event would restart the flush you just stopped: resume it with `mf watch resume` (spec-file-tracking "Pausing ingestion") |
|`load`, `prune`, `rollback` |cannot be stopped: a load leaves the repository unusable until it ends, and the other two are single atomic transactions, with nothing to stop but a rollback of themselves |

Stopping a flush ([[Pausing the watcher]]) is how a mistaken copy or a huge move is kept out of the
database.
